# Netlify `_redirects`. Copied verbatim into the build output by Vite.
#
# SPA fallback: hand every path that is not a real file to index.html, with a
# 200 rather than a redirect, so the URL the visitor typed survives and the
# router decides what it means. `/tools/base64` and a share link's long query
# string both have to arrive intact.
#
# Netlify matches static files FIRST, so this never shadows a real asset. That
# is not an assumption: served through `netlify dev`, /robots.txt, /sw.js and
# /favicon.svg all came back as themselves with their own content types, and
# only /tools/base64 and /nothing-here fell through to the document. A forced
# rewrite (`/*  /index.html  200!`) would have shadowed all of them, which is
# the mistake this rule is one exclamation mark away from.
#
# The 404 is therefore rendered by the app (src/routes/NotFound.tsx), not by a
# static 404.html: only the router knows the route table, and a static page
# could not tell /tools/base64 (real) from /tools/base46 (a typo).
#
# One consequence worth naming: an unknown URL returns 200, not 404. That is
# how every client-routed site behaves and it is what makes deep links work at
# all - the alternative is serving a 404 body to a URL the app can handle.

/*    /index.html   200
